everybody wants to go on holiday and get  back to normal as quickly as possible  but this is reckless we know that the  Delta variant came into this country and  delayed the lifting of some of the  restrictions and caused infections here  we need to make sure that we've got  proper data-driven analysis and that we  look at an international passport for  vaccines and we also know that people  who have had the vaccine of course can  still get the virus so a testing regime  is very important and crucial as well so  I'm very concerned about the  government's announcements by the press  this morning okay which particular part  of it is reckless do you feel because a  lot of Brits want to go on holiday  they've been double jabbed get a week in  the sun after the last 18 months come  back and not and be able to go back to  work rather than having to self-isolate  sure and I completely understand  people's position on that and of course  we want to be able to facilitate that as  quickly as possible but like I say we  know that the vaccine passport isn't an  internationally recognized one we don't  know the circumstances of how that's  going to be administrated and of course  people can still carry mutations and  different levels of the virus so  therefore we believe that at the moment  the government haven't done enough to  make sure that we Safeguard our borders  and we haven't got an internationally  recognized vaccine passport which is  what the government said that they was  working towards so it does feel Reckless  especially when we saw the Delta variant  coming in this country yeah but um  looking at I mean if you're double  jabbed and then the Fortnight afterwards  you are 10 according to the latest  figures just out today you're 10 times  less likely to die from uh covid than  you would be if you had not been  vaccinated at some stage we have to  learn to live with this virus sure and  the vaccine program has been a huge  success and you know the Oxford trials  and everything that we're able to  achieve in this country has been amazing  but actually you can still get the virus  you can still get long covered and it's  so important that we know where the  virus is and whether it mutates or not  so testing is a really important part of  that tool but do you acknowledge that we  will have to live with kobit at some  stage we will have to accept that it is  is within our communities forever yeah  and we're already living with the virus  at the moment and that's why a testing  regime is really crucial about that  because we know the virus will continue  to be prevalent across the UK and  globally but we have to keep an eye on  mutations because the vaccine at the  moment has held up against any of the  mutations but we know what that can do  to our economy if we do get outbreaks of  infections and it causes more problems  in the UK from the 16th of August that's  the date that the government is giving  us date a date and updates is what they  often say that little phrase  um if you've been pinged if you've come  into contact with somebody you've had  who's had covered and you're double jab  then you're still going to be able to go  to work some unions are not happy with  that yeah and we believe that actually  you know we've seen now the virus we  know what the virus is like we've got  more information about that so that's  why testing and ventilation in  workplaces and in our schools is really  important as well so we've said that the  government needs to follow the medical  guidance and the advice and the  scientists and we also need to make sure  sure that that testing regime is there  for people it's free when they need it  and that's a crucial part of dealing  with this virus as well as having those  vaccines so Labor's view is even after  the 16th of August if workers are not  comfortable going into work they feel  anxious about it then they shouldn't  have to now employers should work with  employees and that's what's been  happening across the UK actually and  most of employees have been going into  work and have been supporting that of  course the sage advice has been that  where people can work from home that  that should be possible and again today  I'm announcing around flexible working  that's not just about working from home  but looking at compressed hours as well  so that people can have a real balance  between their family life and their work  life so more people will be able to come  back into the workplace and more people  will be able to work in a flexible way  that will help businesses as well as  help their family so do you think that  covid has changed the landscape and that  we will find some workers not having to  go into the office again I think covert  has changed the landscape that's pretty  clear and what we we've announced today  and what we're saying is that actually  we need to think about that I think  before there was a hesitation from  employers thinking that productivity  would be low or it would be too  strenuous for them to look at working in  different flexible ways but we've seen  that they've been able to do that and  employees have stepped up to the plate  and they've done that as well so now  we're saying well actually why don't we  look at flexible working and having  different compressed hours that work  around your child care that work around  your family lives that will enable more  people to go back into the workplace  into their skilled employment that they  may have and give a good work-life  balance to people which of course is  incredibly important to people and we've  seen that through this pandemic the  importance of family the importance of  friends and being able to be part of  that as well as work  so just to clarify you do envisage your  situation where some people will not go  into the office again they may not and  you know some people may work from home  and of course some people have already  been doing that that a lot of people  